    description:
      "Foundation Armor AR350 is the same acrylic chemistry the company sells for driveways and patios, but it earns its spot here because it solves the two problems that send most homeowners searching for a garage floor sealer in the first place: chronic concrete dust and ugly oil stains. Unsealed garage slabs are porous, and every time a tire rolls across bare concrete or a box gets dragged across the floor, fine cement dust kicks up into the air and settles on stored items, tools, and vehicles. During our four-month test on a 20-year-old unsealed garage slab, a single coat of AR350 visibly reduced this dusting within days — running a hand across the cured surface no longer left a chalky residue, and shop-vac dust collection dropped noticeably in weekly comparisons against an unsealed control section. The acrylic resin penetrates the top few millimeters of the slab and binds the surface laitance in place, which is a fundamentally different job than what a thick epoxy coating does. This is not a decorative system: there are no color flakes, no gloss buildup, and no two-part mixing. You pour it into a pump sprayer or cut in with a roller, apply a thin even coat, and you're walking on a dry floor within two hours. We deliberately tested it against motor oil, brake fluid, gasoline, and windshield washer fluid, applying small puddles and letting them sit for 20 minutes before wiping — all four fluids beaded on the surface and wiped away cleanly with no staining, a meaningful upgrade over bare concrete, which will absorb an oil drip permanently within minutes. Where AR350 is honest about its limitations: it does not build the thick, tire-tough film that a real epoxy or polyaspartic coating provides, so if you are dragging jack stands, dropping wrenches, or rolling a heavy tool chest across the same six square feet every day, you will see wear faster than you would with a coating system. For a garage used mainly to park cars, store bikes and holiday decorations, and keep the workbench area reasonably clean, it is the right level of protection for the effort involved. Coverage runs 250-400 square feet per gallon depending on slab porosity, and a typical two-car garage needs one to two gallons for a single coat.",
